# Whether this hypervisor's guests are running (#257, ADR-0028).
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. ADR-0007 keeps the lab's telemetry in the lab, so a guest that
# dies takes its own monitoring with it and the estate sees a healthy DL360.
# ADR-0028 decides that a guest's RUN STATE is hypervisor state rather than guest
# telemetry, so it may cross — read from `qm`/`pct` on the hypervisor, carrying
# nothing about what any guest is doing.
#
# It closes "the guest died" and deliberately not "the lab stack inside it died";
# the ADR says so and names the firewall pass the second half would need.
#
# HARDENING DELIBERATELY LIGHTER THAN ITS SIBLINGS, and this is a correction.
# The first version carried ProtectSystem=strict and PrivateNetwork=true, copied
# from homelab-pve-version.service where they are fine. `pveversion` reads the
# dpkg database; `qm list` does not. It reads /etc/pve, which is **pmxcfs, a FUSE
# mount**, and may reach pvedaemon over the loopback API — a private mount
# namespace can leave the FUSE mount invisible, and PrivateNetwork gives the unit
# its own loopback that reaches nothing.
#
# The first real run on Saruman reported ZERO guests on a hypervisor running
# `alexander`. So both are dropped here.
#
# This is a hypothesis, not a measurement: VLAN 99 cannot reach VLAN 30 and the
# command cannot be run from where this was written. It is stated that way rather
# than as a finding — if the next run works, the two directives can be
# reintroduced one at a time with evidence, and the collector now fails loudly
# enough to say which one broke it.
#
# EVERY REMAINING DIRECTIVE IS ONE THAT DOES NOT CREATE A MOUNT NAMESPACE.
# ProtectSystem, ProtectHome, PrivateTmp, ReadWritePaths and the ProtectKernel*
# family all do, and any of them can be what hides a FUSE mount from the unit.
# Since the cause cannot be confirmed from here, the whole class is removed
# rather than guessed at one at a time — and they are worth reintroducing
# individually, with a run to prove each, once this reports guests at all.
#
# What is left still bounds the job meaningfully: it cannot gain privileges, it
# cannot be handed a setuid binary, and it runs one command that reads a list.
#
# Root is only for the textfile directory, which is root-owned on an agent host.
# `qm list` needs no privilege of its own.
[Unit]
Description=HomeLab: record which guests this hypervisor is running
Documentation=file:///home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab/docs/runbooks/schedule-maintenance.md
ConditionPathIsDirectory=/var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector
ConditionPathExists=/usr/sbin/qm

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=root
Group=root
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/homelab-collect-guest-state
TimeoutStartSec=120
Restart=no
Nice=10
NoNewPrivileges=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
LockPersonality=true
RestrictRealtime=true
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=homelab-guest-state
